INT. WRESTLING GYM - EMPTY LOCKER ROOM - NIGHT

A concrete locker room beneath a high school gym. Rows of dented lockers. A long bench. A whiteboard with half-erased plays. Fluorescent tubes buzz. A cheap phone sits on the bench, facedown, silent.

No one is here. Rain drums on the roof above. Then footsteps on the stairs.

COACH ODETTE BRANNIGAN, fifties, enters, shaking water from a whistle lanyard. She wears a windbreaker with the school's faded crest. She is soaked and she doesn't care. She crosses to the bench, picks up the phone, studies it, sets it down.

The phone buzzes. She looks at the screen. She does not answer. It goes to voicemail. A recorded voice fills the room.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
Hey, Odette. It's you. It's me. Yesterday you. Listen, you're going to want to quit tonight.

ODETTE
Don't.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
Don't say "don't." You always say "don't." Just listen.

She sits on the bench, elbows on knees.

ODETTE
Twenty-six years. Twenty-six years of whistles, of weigh-ins, of boys crying in showers.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
Of boys crying in showers.

ODETTE
And what do I get? A losing season. A board meeting. A letter.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
The letter said "restructure."

ODETTE
I know what it said. It said, "Thank you for your service, please clear your locker."

She stands, goes to a locker at the end of the row, spins the dial, opens it. It is full of ribbons, photographs, a dented trophy. She takes none of it.

ODETTE (CONT'D)
Eleven state champions. Forty-four all-conference. One kid who made the Olympic trials and cried so hard he forgot his shoes.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
He forgot his shoes, Odette. You drove them to Colorado.

ODETTE
Shut up.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
You drove eleven hours with a pair of shoes on the passenger seat. And he wrestled barefoot because they were the wrong size.

ODETTE
He still won.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
He still won.

She takes a deep breath. She closes the locker. She leans her forehead against it.

ODETTE (CONT'D)
I'm tired.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
I know. That's why you recorded this. So that I would say it to you when you were tired enough to hear.

ODETTE
What do I say to them?

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
Nothing. You just show up. Tomorrow at six. Same as always. Let the board send their letter.

ODETTE
They'll fire me.

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
Probably.

ODETTE
Then why?

COACH ODETTE (V.O.)
(from voicemail)
Because the boys will be there at six. And they're waiting for you to say go.

A pause. The voicemail ends with a soft beep. Odette picks up the phone and holds it to her chest for a long moment. She lays it on the bench. Then she goes to the whiteboard, picks up a marker, and writes in large block letters: "6 AM."

ODETTE (CONT'D)
(to the empty room)
Go.

She turns off the light.
